A||||3||Gold-86SE||Did I do something wrong?||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||06-26-2000||11:50 AM||||<BR>Referencing an '86 v6 4-spd.<P>Saturday evening I was returning home from being out of town. I stopped to fill my gas tank, and when I got back on the road, I began hearing fairly loud noices from the tranny area.<P>Read the trouble shooting in the manuals, and everything indicated low level of tranny oil.  Went out Sunday and sure enough, the oil was not even touching the speed sensor.<P>Went and purchased 5-30 oil, like the book said, and poured in 2 quarts. Yes, 2 quarts. I did not know at the time how much was needed.<P>I poured in one quart and measured the depth with the speed sensor, the oil was barely touching the gear, so I poured in more, measured again, still not enough, poured in the rest of the second quart.  When I measured it that time the oil was pretty close to the top of the sensor, above the two engraved rings which run around the sensor.  Test drove the car, everything seemed fine.<P>Now, this morning when I get ready to go to work, after backing out of the garage, what do I find - a sizable puddle of oil sitting under the tranny.<P>Checked my manuals again, and one of the possibilities was too much oil, in the case. When adding the oil the case was cold to the touch. What do you guys think? Have I messed up something?<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||06-26-2000||12:52 PM||||if you hurt it, it was running it w/o oil that did it. depends allot on how far you went w/o oil and how hard on it you where driving it. If it was a pretty short drive you likely didn't hurt it much. trannys are mor forgiving about such things than motors.<P>sounds like you lost a seal. That's likely why it was so low to begin with. you should have a visible trail of oil coming from someplace under there.<P>(I thought full was when the oil was between the lines myself....... I have an automatic I can never rememeber.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||06-26-2000||01:55 PM||||As I understand the configuration of these transmissions, output shafts aren't supported on the outer edge of the housing, and that they can become loose enough to waste the seal. There's a repair kit that employs a bearing and a new seal that stablizes the shaft and prevents future seal damage. The kits aren't too expensive aand should be available at most auto parts places. Sounds like that might be what happened to you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||06-26-2000||11:49 PM||||<BR>I found after a drive today, that the outer seal was leaking, and leaking something terrible.<P>So tonight I pulled the shaft and right rear apart and replaced the seal, have not finished everything yet, but hopefully this will repair the problem.  And I suppose that I will change the left one as well.<P>I found it quite interesting that the seal that was in it had rtv around the seal. So whoever did it did a half job, delaying the inevitable.  Why mechanics band-aid problems, instead of fix them is beyond me.  Repairs like this really drive me crazy.<BR>||||reg||14
